-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7ded8315-1dbf-5143-8c09-39b574a39a0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W(x)\\ne 0$ for all $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397665} {:answer/id #uuid "703b696a-f747-5010-96b9-919bbf602029" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W(x)=0$ for all $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397666} {:answer/id #uuid "a35cefdf-5d10-56e7-a5d1-a38747aa53bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W(x)=0$ for some but not all $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397667}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "703b696a-f747-5010-96b9-919bbf602029" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W(x)=0$ for all $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397666} :answer-field/id #uuid "02065a3e-a8fd-5833-9e30-70ce022d3cfe" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397664}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0b1f9810-dc22-5af5-9afc-b7fe4c62290d" :question/math-academy-id "q-343295" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0\n$$\n\nLet ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ be solutions to the second-order homogeneous differential equation above, where $Q(x)$ is continuous on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ The Wronskian satisfies $W(0)=0.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.The Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies the following first-order differential equation:\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)={{field-1}}W(x)\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we get\n\n$$\nW={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nNow, using the fact that $W(0)=0,$ we obtain \n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=3.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-3W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-3W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-3\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -3\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =-3x+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{e}^{-3x} \\\\ W & =C{e}^{-3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(0)=0$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(0) & =C{e}^{-3\\cdot 0} \\\\ 0 & =C\\cdot 1 \\\\ C & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $W(x)=0\\text{ for all }x\\in R.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397668 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4733fab7-18ca-5336-9aba-53212ddbf87b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First Order ODEs Given the Fourier Series of the Forcing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af7b840f-2e0c-52c0-b79f-c78277457040" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{54}{n\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+81)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397670} {:answer/id #uuid "addf1d04-69f0-5452-bba4-8900e12fa907" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{162}{n\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+81)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397671} {:answer/id #uuid "8626a439-85ec-5b4a-9bb1-b4485f902170" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{162}{n\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+81)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397672}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8626a439-85ec-5b4a-9bb1-b4485f902170" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{162}{n\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+81)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397672} :answer-field/id #uuid "865ba785-fbd6-5a07-957c-16cb2c1df477" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397669} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a53b81f-d126-598c-8b37-6e225ea3cb17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{6}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+81)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397674} {:answer/id #uuid "f5abb3ad-0dba-5b32-98f6-df1e57e1d85e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+81}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397675} {:answer/id #uuid "0102c81a-3dd5-541b-9257-ca77f5322bb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{18}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+81}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397676}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0102c81a-3dd5-541b-9257-ca77f5322bb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{18}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+81}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397676} :answer-field/id #uuid "91f55656-129c-5fe5-8079-32fba851d88e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397673} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22623b70-5aab-52fc-9402-941db3c703fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397678} {:answer/id #uuid "ee445dc1-2657-5591-8683-e95785717f43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397679} {:answer/id #uuid "d51d9118-2abe-5ef4-9ada-c8a08d3b5d43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397680} {:answer/id #uuid "d01d3c08-0c36-57bc-8823-3c469f701c68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397681}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "22623b70-5aab-52fc-9402-941db3c703fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397678} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d625df2-64a2-5d5b-b5dc-aa82f78979a2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397677}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "15f5a0b9-1297-5188-a047-103209518fed" :question/math-academy-id "q-343855" :question/problem "Select the missing parts in a particular solution of the differential equation ${y}^{′}-3y=f(x),$ where the Fourier series of the $6$-periodic forcing function is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼-2+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nThe Fourier series of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}∼{{field-1}} & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-2}}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-3}}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We assume that a solution of our equation has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\  & ∼0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\cdot (-\\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}))+{b}_{n}\\cdot \\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{p}^{′}$ and ${y}_{p}$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\  & \\,-3(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\  & \\,\\,=-2+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -3\\cdot \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-3{a}_{n})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & \\,\\,+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-3{b}_{n})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,=-2+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, equating the free terms and the coefficients next to sines and cosines, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-3\\cdot \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}=-2 \\\\ \\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-3{a}_{n}=0 \\\\ -\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-3{b}_{n}=\\frac{6}{n\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, ${a}_{0}=\\frac{4}{3},$ which means the constant term is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nNext, from the second equation, ${b}_{n}=\\frac{9{a}_{n}}{n\\pi }.$ Substituting this into the third equation, gives\n\n$$\n-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-3(\\frac{9{a}_{n}}{n\\pi })=\\frac{6}{n\\pi }\\,⇒\\,{a}_{n}=-\\frac{18}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+81}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for ${a}_{n}$ with $n\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{n} & =\\frac{9{a}_{n}}{n\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{n\\pi }\\cdot (-\\frac{18}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+81}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{162}{n\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+81)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}∼\\frac{2}{3} & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,-\\frac{18}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+81}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,-\\frac{162}{n\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+81)}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397682 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4733fab7-18ca-5336-9aba-53212ddbf87b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First Order ODEs Given the Fourier Series of the Forcing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f5beaf63-a24e-5096-a60c-b0469d5c3b91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397684} {:answer/id #uuid "abc5583a-c2d4-5270-9535-e8dc76611c8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397685} {:answer/id #uuid "20b43c86-9df5-5ea5-b8db-701f1d2fcb5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397686} {:answer/id #uuid "f8b221ad-c466-515a-9758-208b0b79820b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397687}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "abc5583a-c2d4-5270-9535-e8dc76611c8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397685} :answer-field/id #uuid "dfa212b9-4a9e-5ed1-977d-8a0f92f187bb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397683} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c55a33cd-7217-5f5d-aae1-44a553c062e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397689} {:answer/id #uuid "68fd5497-22fd-5e95-baa6-a3fb2a651897" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{30(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397690} {:answer/id #uuid "9261cac5-a1b2-5b9e-85af-f8f060b21c0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{6(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397691}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c55a33cd-7217-5f5d-aae1-44a553c062e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397689} :answer-field/id #uuid "685da459-566a-5cb9-907e-c163e95f44e3" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397688} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52840280-13ca-54da-b4d4-afd74b42c883" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{18(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397693} {:answer/id #uuid "7120f89a-088f-5258-8c2d-358cc96f6e1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{90(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397694} {:answer/id #uuid "15b8dddf-399a-5ce8-a3a7-717ad28083fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{90(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397695}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7120f89a-088f-5258-8c2d-358cc96f6e1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{90(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397694} :answer-field/id #uuid "615c3feb-87ab-595d-bd3c-9d3dd60c0bff" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397692}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b2c46dfd-7116-546d-b775-da0e3f310626" :question/math-academy-id "q-343860" :question/problem "Select the missing parts in a particular solution of the differential equation ${y}^{′}-5y=f(x),$ where the Fourier series of the $6$-periodic forcing function is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼-5+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nThe Fourier series of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}∼{{field-1}} & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-2}}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-3}}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We assume that a solution of our equation has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\  & ∼0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\cdot (-\\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}))+{b}_{n}\\cdot \\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{p}^{′}$ and ${y}_{p}$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\  & \\,-5(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\  & \\,\\,=-5+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -5\\cdot \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-5{a}_{n})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & \\,\\,+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-5{b}_{n})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,=-5+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, equating the free terms and the coefficients next to sines and cosines, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-5\\cdot \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}=-5 \\\\ \\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-5{a}_{n}=0 \\\\ -\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-5{b}_{n}=\\frac{2(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, ${a}_{0}=2,$ which means the constant term is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nNext, from the second equation, ${b}_{n}=\\frac{15{a}_{n}}{n\\pi }.$ Substituting this into the third equation, gives\n\n$$\n-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-5(\\frac{15{a}_{n}}{n\\pi })=\\frac{2(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\,⇒\\,{a}_{n}=\\frac{6(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for ${a}_{n}$ with $n\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{n} & =\\frac{15{a}_{n}}{n\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{n\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{6(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225} \\\\  & =\\frac{90(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}∼1 & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{6(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{90(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397696 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e42e65b-6e26-5a3f-9275-25a291e13138" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Resonant Harmonics"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26abab5a-e0d3-5a63-b7b9-8c8b067c042b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397698} {:answer/id #uuid "1bc27d3d-2228-5745-9f75-6ad665b3203a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397699} {:answer/id #uuid "c26489de-65c7-593c-9bc1-121a32b98a82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397700} {:answer/id #uuid "efa4633c-d9b6-5177-bd10-dd0e9bfa3eb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397701}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "efa4633c-d9b6-5177-bd10-dd0e9bfa3eb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397701} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d3c5e72-c342-555b-a87c-e14e208988df" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397697} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bc221971-12de-52c7-8478-0068ddd7a170" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{54\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397703} {:answer/id #uuid "fe05ab27-ffe9-519c-a245-1e3105109d19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{x}{108\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397704} {:answer/id #uuid "96ad6016-80ff-5081-b817-514fd1499c89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{108\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397705} {:answer/id #uuid "0e910e33-e3b2-5731-a6d5-5a16e1e9ad14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{216\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397706}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "96ad6016-80ff-5081-b817-514fd1499c89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{108\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397705} :answer-field/id #uuid "9fa9bba7-0c1d-559d-9dd6-0cd8ed6cec73" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397702}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d694cb78-fd17-5d72-bd5e-fb91875a20a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-343891" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation ${y}^{″}+36{\\pi }^{2}y=f(x),$ where the $1$-periodic forcing function is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below regarding the resonant component of a particular solution of the equation.\n\nThe natural frequency of the system matches the frequency of the {{field-1}} harmonic of the forcing function. \n\nConsequently, the particular solution contains a resonant term of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{3}}={{field-2}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We begin by identifying resonance. The differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+36{\\pi }^{2}y=f(x)\n$$\n\nhas a natural frequency of \n\n$$\n\\omega =\\sqrt{36{\\pi }^{2}}=6\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe frequencies of the forcing harmonics for a $1$-periodic function $(L=\\frac{1}{2})$ are \n\n$$\n{\\omega }_{n}=\\frac{n\\pi }{(\\frac{1}{2})}=2n\\pi .\n$$\n\nFor $n=3$, we have ${\\omega }_{3}=6\\pi .$ Since ${\\omega }_{3}=\\omega ,$ the $n=3$ harmonic of the forcing function causes resonance.\n\nFor the $n=3$ harmonic, the forcing term is \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x)=\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x).\n$$\n\nBecause this frequency matches the natural frequency, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{3}}=x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x)).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{{p}_{3}}^{′} & =(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x))+x(-6\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x)+6\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x)) \\\\ {y}_{{p}_{3}}^{″} & =-12\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x)+12\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x)-36{\\pi }^{2}x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{{p}_{3}}^{″}$ and ${y}_{{p}_{3}}$ into the equation ${y}^{″}+36{\\pi }^{2}y=\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x):$\n\n$$\n[-12\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x)+12\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x)-36{\\pi }^{2}{y}_{{p}_{3}}]+36{\\pi }^{2}{y}_{{p}_{3}}=\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x)\n$$\n\nThe resonant terms involving $x$ cancel out, leaving\n\n$$\n-12\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x)+12\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x)=\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-12\\pi A=0 \\\\ 12\\pi B=\\frac{1}{9}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}A=0 \\\\ B=\\frac{1}{108\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resonant component of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{3}}=\\frac{x}{108\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397707 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e42e65b-6e26-5a3f-9275-25a291e13138" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Resonant Harmonics"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60cf0d89-49e8-5b67-80c1-e7d2d7a3ffb4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{x}{8\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397709} {:answer/id #uuid "a1876296-7211-55be-aba4-bfdcc1a01df6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{16\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397710} {:answer/id #uuid "09aff7b4-dbdd-58ab-8229-b25ef4b4ba18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{4\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397711} {:answer/id #uuid "fe23c02e-5cbf-556d-b7f3-55b796e71bfc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{8\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397712}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fe23c02e-5cbf-556d-b7f3-55b796e71bfc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{8\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397712} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef63660a-274c-5dd7-9740-8afb5e0d6094" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397708} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b222e16-92bf-5e7b-a075-99b8093081a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397714} {:answer/id #uuid "e3cd2371-beae-59d9-8d7e-94aae5b22c28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397715} {:answer/id #uuid "964c305f-0261-5625-959d-d4db38c740ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397716} {:answer/id #uuid "63032ea2-0b98-59b3-8f8b-f8122510525d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397717}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "964c305f-0261-5625-959d-d4db38c740ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397716} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f3873a4-d6a5-52d5-8df1-9961c00d5836" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397713}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dd94ef93-5daf-566f-92be-7aef938749ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-343899" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation ${y}^{″}+{\\pi }^{2}y=f(x),$ where the $4$-periodic forcing function is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼5+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below regarding the resonant component of a particular solution of the equation.\n\nThe natural frequency of the system matches the frequency of the {{field-1}} harmonic of the forcing function. \n\nConsequently, the particular solution contains a resonant term of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{2}}={{field-2}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We begin by identifying resonance. The differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\pi }^{2}y=f(x)\n$$\n\nhas a natural frequency of \n\n$$\n\\omega =\\sqrt{{\\pi }^{2}}=\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe frequencies of the forcing harmonics for a $4$-periodic function $(L=2)$ are \n\n$$\n{\\omega }_{n}=\\frac{n\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nFor $n=2$, we have ${\\omega }_{2}=\\pi .$ Since ${\\omega }_{2}=\\omega ,$ the $n=2$ harmonic of the forcing function causes resonance.\n\nFor the $n=2$ harmonic, the forcing term is \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x)=\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x).\n$$\n\nBecause this frequency matches the natural frequency, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{2}}=x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{{p}_{2}}^{′} & =(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x))+x(-\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)) \\\\ {y}_{{p}_{2}}^{″} & =-2\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+2\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)-{\\pi }^{2}x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{{p}_{2}}^{″}$ and ${y}_{{p}_{2}}$ into the equation ${y}^{″}+{\\pi }^{2}y=\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x):$\n\n$$\n[-2\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+2\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)-{\\pi }^{2}{y}_{{p}_{2}}]+{\\pi }^{2}{y}_{{p}_{2}}=\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)\n$$\n\nThe resonant terms involving $x$ cancel out, leaving\n\n$$\n-2\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+2\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)=\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-2\\pi A=0 \\\\ 2\\pi B=\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}A=0 \\\\ B=\\frac{1}{8\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resonant component of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{2}}=\\frac{x}{8\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397718 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e42e65b-6e26-5a3f-9275-25a291e13138" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Resonant Harmonics"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "718e9566-c7c9-5a9a-b62c-206f45871768" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397720} {:answer/id #uuid "4123d653-3eb6-56f8-9a54-2ef04840a633" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397721} {:answer/id #uuid "5c2e6e0a-4098-5507-86d3-f9eae8b3dec8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397722} {:answer/id #uuid "d77b424d-712f-5538-af48-9fa7a0208031" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397723}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d77b424d-712f-5538-af48-9fa7a0208031" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397723} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c917125-cff6-58cf-bd8e-53fff9587ed6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397719} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d4a9dd1f-56de-5787-b2b5-60a35b059c5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{27\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397725} {:answer/id #uuid "216bc2a3-5817-55ad-92ea-6ce57b3a3498" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{x}{54\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397726} {:answer/id #uuid "17e5708c-ca06-5b4e-bd8a-6d23c5265ddf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{x}{27\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397727} {:answer/id #uuid "fd5c93f6-1ed7-54a8-a40e-3a422b0a0168" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{x}{9\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397728}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "17e5708c-ca06-5b4e-bd8a-6d23c5265ddf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{x}{27\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397727} :answer-field/id #uuid "69b53d84-a0ad-59e0-b26e-795d6cf7c7fe" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397724}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "68ceb45a-31c0-520d-b3cb-f81e2d91b31a" :question/math-academy-id "q-343910" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation ${y}^{″}+9{\\pi }^{2}y=f(x),$ where the $2$-periodic forcing function is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼1+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below regarding the resonant component of a particular solution of the equation.\n\nThe natural frequency of the system matches the frequency of the {{field-1}} harmonic of the forcing function. \n\nConsequently, the particular solution contains a resonant term of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{3}}={{field-2}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We begin by identifying resonance. The differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+9{\\pi }^{2}y=f(x)\n$$\n\nhas a natural frequency of \n\n$$\n\\omega =\\sqrt{9{\\pi }^{2}}=3\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe frequencies of the forcing harmonics for a $2$-periodic function $(L=1)$ are \n\n$$\n{\\omega }_{n}=\\frac{n\\pi }{1}=n\\pi .\n$$\n\nFor $n=3$, we have ${\\omega }_{3}=3\\pi .$ Since ${\\omega }_{3}=\\omega ,$ the $n=3$ harmonic of the forcing function causes resonance.\n\nFor the $n=3$ harmonic, the forcing term is \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x)=\\frac{2}{9}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x).\n$$\n\nBecause this frequency matches the natural frequency, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{3}}=x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x)).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{{p}_{3}}^{′} & =(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x))+x(-3\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x)+3\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x)) \\\\ {y}_{{p}_{3}}^{″} & =-6\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x)+6\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x)-9{\\pi }^{2}x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{{p}_{3}}^{″}$ and ${y}_{{p}_{3}}$ into the equation ${y}^{″}+9{\\pi }^{2}y=\\frac{2}{9}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x):$\n\n$$\n[-6\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x)+6\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x)-9{\\pi }^{2}{y}_{{p}_{3}}]+9{\\pi }^{2}{y}_{{p}_{3}}=\\frac{2}{9}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x)\n$$\n\nThe resonant terms involving $x$ cancel out, leaving\n\n$$\n-6\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x)+6\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x)=\\frac{2}{9}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-6\\pi A=\\frac{2}{9} \\\\ 6\\pi B=0\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}A=-\\frac{1}{27\\pi } \\\\ B=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resonant component of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{3}}=-\\frac{x}{27\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397729 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f542453-9758-55e6-93c6-6bfd2139ec45" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Tangent Line Approximation and Classifying it as an Underestimate or Overestimate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d40eb58-2655-5469-940e-f80c613a366a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397731} {:answer/id #uuid "cd504e90-e5b7-56c6-964b-40f89c7b3493" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397732} {:answer/id #uuid "a07cbcd9-56d0-586c-aa21-1a80b6af5ef6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397733} {:answer/id #uuid "9fc20b50-1f58-592c-8612-6b49865b0664" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397734} {:answer/id #uuid "286353c9-a32e-56eb-8992-b6c84200e329" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397735}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a07cbcd9-56d0-586c-aa21-1a80b6af5ef6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397733} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a9687f0-3dbc-5f03-bc8c-36c768da7028" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397730} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "92b8f43d-f4d5-509d-8b12-68825b47ce0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "overestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397737} {:answer/id #uuid "ef65b10b-e65d-5550-b964-4477632fe18b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "underestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397738}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "92b8f43d-f4d5-509d-8b12-68825b47ce0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "overestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397737} :answer-field/id #uuid "52b792d3-0847-501a-907c-2ebe956b9ca4" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397736}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fb9ed8ae-2017-567d-8d5e-a0f446be0601" :question/math-academy-id "q-346720" :question/problem "The graph of the function $y=f(x)$ passes through $(0,2)$ and is concave down on the interval $(-2,2).$ If ${f}^{′}(0)=0.5,$ estimate the value of $f(0.4)$ using the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=0$ and determine whether the approximation is an underestimate or overestimate.$\\,f(0.4)≈$ {{field-1}} and the approximation is an {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=0$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(0)+{f}^{′}(0)(x-0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph of the function passes through the point $(0,2),$ so we have $f(0)=2.$ Also, we're told that ${f}^{′}(0)=0.5.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =2+0.5x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can approximate $f(0.4)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=0.4.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0.4) & ≈L(0.4) \\\\  & =2+0.5(0.4) \\\\  & =2.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the function is concave down on the interval $(-2,2),$ the linear approximation $L(x)$ is above the graph near $x=0,$ and therefore we have an $\\text{overestimate}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397739 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7ac34a18-053d-5eab-8c2c-bd0f15658186" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order Linear ODEs Using Variation of Parameters"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f63dcef-6e20-5403-a462-b25bca77f0df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397741} {:answer/id #uuid "73133d27-3932-5ef7-a71f-df7f08b40324" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397742} {:answer/id #uuid "a5984a2b-a6e3-5dd3-95eb-041759b9f99e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397743} {:answer/id #uuid "d1e2e547-0e12-580f-ae06-681f5cc502ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397744} {:answer/id #uuid "1f4522a5-e869-5e67-add6-a59161b129cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397745}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f4522a5-e869-5e67-add6-a59161b129cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397745} :answer-field/id #uuid "69acc76f-e6dd-53c9-a2d7-4929026fb4c8" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397740} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ea685ad9-8dcf-55b7-a739-bd4306d3c8e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397747} {:answer/id #uuid "7aab73a2-9bd4-53e3-9b2e-4069fdbada5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397748} {:answer/id #uuid "8c168341-7f63-5ed7-bd61-ac7d0c8ae880" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397749} {:answer/id #uuid "513dc7fc-70e9-5782-87d8-c29a8bbc9061" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397750} {:answer/id #uuid "84134dc2-eddb-5150-b0d9-6e42375be127" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397751}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7aab73a2-9bd4-53e3-9b2e-4069fdbada5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397748} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e11331f-f12c-56ec-abc2-41fb74d0907d" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397746} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c7b312d-000a-53d2-b64a-ae56b169c7e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397753} {:answer/id #uuid "1236b2ee-c79e-5b48-af53-571e33ebaf43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397754} {:answer/id #uuid "bfb5d257-83c4-5b1d-bab7-bb3092c22daa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397755} {:answer/id #uuid "d3eaaabd-442d-53ac-81ea-8a2a5fd0cca1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397756} {:answer/id #uuid "e84d8a1c-818b-5f53-a0a3-e2ce9dab7827" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397757}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1236b2ee-c79e-5b48-af53-571e33ebaf43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397754} :answer-field/id #uuid "3394c0e8-a1fd-5164-b5fe-2278c6eec3bf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397752}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "efe05efa-2761-5181-aebf-f550bba3f8de" :question/math-academy-id "q-352890" :question/problem "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3y={e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-3{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{3x})}^{′}-3(u{e}^{3x}) & ={e}^{2x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{3x}+3u{e}^{3x}-3u{e}^{3x} & ={e}^{2x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{3x}+\\cancel{3u{e}^{3x}}-\\cancel{3u{e}^{3x}} & ={e}^{2x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{3x} & ={e}^{2x} \\\\ {u}^{′} & ={e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & ={e}^{-x} \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =-{e}^{-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{3x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{3x} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-x}\\cdot {e}^{3x} \\\\  & =-{e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397758 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7ac34a18-053d-5eab-8c2c-bd0f15658186" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order Linear ODEs Using Variation of Parameters"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36dc406b-5149-5aeb-99f4-c422c56adc09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{3x}\\int {e}^{-3x}(1-4x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397760} {:answer/id #uuid "11154d93-5ccc-5c68-a857-aee315edeb48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{3x}\\int {e}^{3x}(1-4x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397761} {:answer/id #uuid "23e73190-9abd-5014-8776-a3e439817b2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-3x}\\int {e}^{-3x}(1-4x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397762} {:answer/id #uuid "2db97ceb-c880-55bd-9dad-60e6eed828e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-3x}\\int {e}^{3x}(1-4x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397763}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2db97ceb-c880-55bd-9dad-60e6eed828e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-3x}\\int {e}^{3x}(1-4x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397763} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a467a6f-a5c6-5790-ac4d-f97c7812ce6a" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397759} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10c182f9-383c-58da-882f-395c04dd6b90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int {e}^{-3x}(1-4x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397765} {:answer/id #uuid "a1e5a89f-4546-53e1-a022-5e7739f83b1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int {e}^{3x}(1-4x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397766} {:answer/id #uuid "1ec37a94-cd94-5e1f-98c5-54bc9d2c9cff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int {e}^{3x}(4x-1)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397767} {:answer/id #uuid "dd232a56-f4cc-56fc-b884-30cf0430eb44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int {e}^{3x}(1+4x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397768}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a1e5a89f-4546-53e1-a022-5e7739f83b1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int {e}^{3x}(1-4x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397766} :answer-field/id #uuid "4fff85ae-4624-593f-9e66-64f227957388" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397764} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0512b396-796a-5a66-9e01-233e25cac296" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397770} {:answer/id #uuid "a5e91fdf-1722-5c86-aba4-e7001a74c175" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397771} {:answer/id #uuid "81b3d8a7-3cff-52cb-8e4e-a47b128cf79e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397772} {:answer/id #uuid "37921492-3c46-559e-816c-ff8ee78351ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397773} {:answer/id #uuid "e2ff85d2-c6bc-5e83-bcd4-df22d2bc5e11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397774}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "37921492-3c46-559e-816c-ff8ee78351ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397773} :answer-field/id #uuid "48b6e071-99a9-577f-a558-4259b093492e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397769}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d21173e6-c5c3-5364-8e5b-d645bc7f05bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-352895" :question/problem "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+3y=1-4x.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+3{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{-3x})}^{′}+3(u{e}^{-3x}) & =1-4x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-3x}-3u{e}^{-3x}+3u{e}^{-3x} & =1-4x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-3x}-\\cancel{3u{e}^{-3x}}+\\cancel{3u{e}^{-3x}} & =1-4x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-3x} & =1-4x \\\\ {u}^{′} & ={e}^{3x}(1-4x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & ={e}^{3x}(1-4x) \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{3x}(1-4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =\\int {e}^{3x}(1-4x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{-3x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{-3x} \\\\  & =\\int {e}^{3x}(1-4x)\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {e}^{-3x} \\\\  & ={e}^{-3x}\\int {e}^{3x}(1-4x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397775 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "126ffd6c-f5a9-564f-935f-398a126d2cbd" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a First-Order Linear Equation From a d'Alembert Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7ed958e4-1f96-524f-b3de-110ff91c56eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{t}^{2}}{{t}^{2}-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397777} {:answer/id #uuid "2965ac18-e5e8-50d6-a1d8-51c095517414" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{t({t}^{2}-1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397778} {:answer/id #uuid "1716108b-afba-5b8e-8f20-72faa3deabf0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{t}{{t}^{2}-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397779} {:answer/id #uuid "352ae16b-e108-578f-a46d-867b64df0c48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397780}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1716108b-afba-5b8e-8f20-72faa3deabf0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{t}{{t}^{2}-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397779} :answer-field/id #uuid "66f88564-b8cd-591d-a785-13a83fdfb6b9" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397776} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46c4364f-4fa5-52be-b64d-080c7d0e12da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{t({t}^{2}-1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397782} {:answer/id #uuid "52293ede-63c0-5c50-a1e8-a410e4858c4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}({t}^{2}-1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397783} {:answer/id #uuid "e32c59ab-db42-5218-85ae-4fb5278cdf4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{t}{{t}^{2}-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397784} {:answer/id #uuid "7790bcee-04c3-594b-bb1b-33e4a4b043f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397785}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46c4364f-4fa5-52be-b64d-080c7d0e12da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{t({t}^{2}-1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397782} :answer-field/id #uuid "b539044b-f263-566b-a583-7f00bd13bba1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397781}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "01283c40-bda7-59b4-8f16-b320c423d254" :question/math-academy-id "q-365387" :question/problem "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x\\cdot \\frac{1}{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nUsing the substitution $t=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ the equation can be transformed to a first-order linear equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t).\n$$\n\nFind the functions $P$ and $Q.$\n\n$\\,\\,P(t)=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,Q(t)=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nIn our case, we wish to transform the equation\n\n$$\ny=x\\cdot \\frac{1}{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\ninto a first-order linear equation for $x=x(t).$ We proceed in three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Substitute, and differentiate with respect to $x.$\n\nWe have the substitution\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny=x\\cdot \\frac{1}{t}+t.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\cdot \\frac{1}{t}+t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the sum and product rules and using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\cdot \\frac{1}{t})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(t) \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{t}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)+x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{t})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(t) \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{t}-\\frac{x}{{t}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Isolate the derivative and take its reciprocal.\n\nWe isolate $\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t-\\frac{1}{t} & =-\\frac{x}{{t}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ \\frac{{t}^{2}-1}{t} & =(\\frac{{t}^{2}-x}{{t}^{2}})\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ \\frac{t({t}^{2}-1)}{{t}^{2}-x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{t({t}^{2}-1)}{{t}^{2}-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by taking the reciprocal of each side, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{{t}^{2}-x}{t({t}^{2}-1)}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Express the derivative as a first-order linear equation.\n\nFinally, we write this in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t)\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{{t}^{2}-x}{t({t}^{2}-1)} \\\\ t({t}^{2}-1)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & ={t}^{2}-x \\\\ t({t}^{2}-1)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+x & ={t}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{x}{t({t}^{2}-1)} & =\\frac{{t}^{2}}{t({t}^{2}-1)} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{x}{t({t}^{2}-1)} & =\\frac{t}{{t}^{2}-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{1}{t({t}^{2}-1)},\\,Q(t)=\\frac{t}{{t}^{2}-1}.\n$$"}]}
